@page "/products"
@implements IDisposable
@inject CartState Cart

<PageTitle>Products · BevTools</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <p class="bb-overline">Scenario · Rendering</p>
    <h1>Products</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A searchable list of 120 products, built the way lists often are at first: a state container that notifies on
        every change, a list that re-renders on every notification, and rows that never override
        <code>ShouldRender</code>. Each keystroke costs a render of every row - and, with search on type, an HTTP request.
        Three more problems hide on this page: data loaded twice, a component that is never freed, and a fix to measure.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The product list</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Type in the search box. <code>FilterBar</code> writes to <code>FilterState</code>, which raises
        <code>Changed</code> without coalescing; <code>ProductList</code> re-renders, and every <code>ProductRow</code>
        with it. <em>Add</em> puts a product in the cart, a named change of <code>CartState</code>. The line above the
        search box is <code>CategorySummary</code>, which loads the products a second time; <em>price alert</em> shows a
        <code>PriceAlert</code> that subscribes to <code>FilterState</code> and never unsubscribes.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span><RendererStatus /></div>

        <div class="bb-toolbar">
            <label class="bb-check"><input type="checkbox" @bind="_searchOnType" /> search the API on every keystroke (a burst of overlapping requests)</label>
            <label class="bb-check"><input type="checkbox" @bind="_priceAlert" /> price alert (hiding it leaks it: problem 16)</label>
            <label class="bb-check"><input type="checkbox" @bind="_skipUnchangedRows" /> the fix: rows skip renders that would change nothing</label>
            <span class="bb-spacer"></span>
            <span class="bb-muted">Cart: @Cart.ItemCount items, @Cart.Total.ToString("C") — see BevTools → State → CartState</span>
        </div>

        @if (_priceAlert)
        {
            <PriceAlert />
        }
        <CategorySummary />
        <FilterBar />
        <ProductList SearchOnType="_searchOnType" SkipUnchangedRows="_skipUnchangedRows" />
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Profiler: what one keystroke costs</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Open the <strong>Profiler</strong>, press <em>Reset</em>, type one character and tick <em>since reset</em>; tick
        <em>by type</em> to see all the rows as one line - what 120 <code>ProductRow</code>s cost together, and that every
        one of their renders was a parent re-render.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><em>Recent render batches</em> lists one batch per keystroke with how many components it rendered, how long building and diffing took, how many DOM edits it made and what triggered it. Select one: its cost tree puts <code>ProductRow ×120 instances</code> under <code>ProductList</code>, each with its own time and its subtree's; <em>Time</em> draws the same renders on a time axis, in the order the renderer ran them.</li>
        <li><strong>Diagnostics</strong> names the pattern: a <em>render cascade</em> - a keystroke's batch renders the list and every row it shows - and <em>renders driven only by the parent</em> for the rows. Drag the max price slider and a <em>render storm</em> on <code>ProductList</code> joins them: every step of the slider is a notification.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Timeline: from the keystroke to the batch</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Each <em>oninput → FilterBar (@@bind)</em> event is followed by a <code>FilterState</code> change (the demo's
        extension shows that store in the State panel) and one batch with 121 renders. Select the event and the Timeline
        lists what it <em>caused</em>; select one of those renders and <em>caused by</em> leads back to the keystroke.
        The chips above the list show or hide each kind of entry.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Components: what the search box runs</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Press <strong>⌖ Pick</strong> in the panel header and click the search box: the inspector opens on <code>FilterBar</code>. Its <em>Event handlers &amp; callbacks</em>, read from its latest render, list each element with the event it handles and the method that runs - <code>@@bind-value → FilterBar.OnQuery</code> for the search box - each with a link to its line in <code>FilterBar.razor</code>.</li>
        <li>Select <code>ProductList</code>: the same section lists the <code>OnAdd</code> callback it passes to every row, and the method it runs (<code>CartState.Add</code>).</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Network: a request per keystroke</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Tick <em>search the API on every keystroke</em> and type quickly. The <strong>Network</strong> panel fills with
        <code>GET /api/search?q=…</code> requests, each triggered by an <em>oninput</em> event (the Trigger column), none
        cancelled, each answer free to overwrite a newer one. <strong>Diagnostics</strong> reports a <em>burst of
        requests to one endpoint</em>: how many in how little time, the event that sent them, and - when the answers are
        slow enough to overlap - how many started while the previous one was still running.
    </p>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        On a Server circuit, <strong>Network → WebSockets</strong> shows what each keystroke costs on the wire: every
        <code>JS.RenderBatch</code> the circuit sent, its size - about 100 KB while all 120 rows re-render - and how
        long the browser took to apply it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Runtime: the products are loaded twice</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Reload this tab. The server prerenders the page, then it becomes interactive, and <code>CategorySummary</code>
        loads the products in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>, which runs both times. <strong>Runtime →
        Prerendering</strong> shows what the server rendered, how long it took and which requests it made;
        <strong>Diagnostics</strong> reports <em>Data fetched twice: while prerendering and when interactive</em>.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Runtime: a hidden alert that is never freed</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Tick and untick <em>price alert</em> a few times. Each <code>PriceAlert</code> subscribed to the scoped
        <code>FilterState</code> with a lambda and never unsubscribed, so the store keeps every one alive.
        <strong>Runtime → Disposed components still alive → Collect garbage and check</strong> lists them, with what
        holds each one: <em>FilterState (injected) → Changed: lambda in PriceAlert.OnInitialized</em>. The demo
        extension's <strong>Store</strong> tab shows the same leak from the store's side: one more subscriber per alert.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Measure the fix</h3>
    <ol class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>In the Profiler press <em>Reset</em>, type <em>lamp</em>, then <em>About → Export session (JSON)</em>: that file is the recording before the fix.</li>
        <li>Tick <em>the fix: rows skip renders that would change nothing</em>, press <em>Reset</em> and type <em>lamp</em> again.</li>
        <li><em>Profiler → Compare…</em> with the file: <code>ProductRow</code> is <em>improved</em>, with its renders before and after. <em>Copy as Markdown</em> puts the comparison in a pull request; in CI, <code>bevtools compare before.json after.json</code> does the same with two files.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">The other fixes to try in code:</p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Debounce <code>FilterState</code>, so a burst of keystrokes is one notification.</li>
        <li>Pass a <code>CancellationToken</code> to the search and cancel it on the next keystroke.</li>
        <li>Carry <code>CategorySummary</code>'s list over from prerendering with <code>[PersistentState]</code> (.NET 10) or <code>PersistentComponentState</code>.</li>
        <li>Keep <code>PriceAlert</code>'s handler in a field, implement <code>IDisposable</code> and unsubscribe in <code>Dispose</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="problems#problem-2" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 2 →</a>
        <a href="problems#problem-3" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 3 →</a>
        <a href="problems#problem-11" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 11 →</a>
        <a href="problems#problem-16" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 16 →</a>
        <a href="problems#problem-21" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 21 →</a>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private bool _searchOnType;
    private bool _priceAlert;
    private bool _skipUnchangedRows;

    // The Add buttons belong to ProductList, so only it re-renders after a click; the cart summary above lives here.
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Cart.Changed += OnCartChanged;

    private void OnCartChanged(BevStateChangeInfo change) => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose() => Cart.Changed -= OnCartChanged;
}
